{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310718,"nonce":"+cas/9DrTflZiOspZFLr3bBn2wo=","title":"UNFIXED at the stdlib layer: `StringMap\u003cT\u003e.get(missing_key)` returns raw runtime `0` instead of boxed `Null\u003cT\u003e`. So `map.get(key) == null` ...","message":"For any `StringMap\u003cInt\u003e`, `IntMap\u003cInt\u003e` — or anything declared `Null\u003cT\u003e`\nwhere the runtime returns a raw integer:\n\n```haxe\nvar m = new StringMap\u003cInt\u003e();\ntrace(m.get(\"missing\"));          // 0   (expected null)\ntrace(m.get(\"missing\") == null);  // false (expected true)\n```\n\n`BPETokenizer.encode` SIGBUSed in `nue/examples/tiny-transformer`\nbecause of exactly this. `rankOf` did:\n\n```haxe\nvar rank = rankIndex.get(left + right);\nreturn (rank == null) ? -1 : rank;\n```\n\n…which returned `0` for missing keys, so the merge loop indexed\n`merges[0]` on an empty array and crashed.\n\n\n`runtime/src/haxe_sys.rs:3471` — `haxe_stringmap_get` returns `u64`\nwith `0u64` for missing keys:\n\n```rust\n/// Returns 0 if key doesn't exist (caller should use exists() to distinguish)\npub extern \"C\" fn haxe_stringmap_get(map_ptr: *mut HaxeStringMap, ...) -\u003e u64 {\n    ...\n    map.map.get(\u0026key_str).copied().unwrap_or(0)\n    ...\n}\n```\n\n`compiler/src/stdlib/runtime_mapping.rs:2448` maps `StringMap.get` as\n`returns: raw_value` — the compiler does **not** wrap the result in\nboxed `Null\u003cT\u003e`. So a user variable typed `Null\u003cInt\u003e` ends up holding\nthe raw `0`, and the `== null` comparison against an unboxed integer\nis unconditionally false.\n\n`IntMap`, `ObjectMap`, and other map types likely have the same shape.\n\n\nIn nue, replaced every `m.get(k) == null` pattern with an explicit\n`if (!m.exists(k)) return -1; m.get(k)`. Three call sites:\n\n- `nue/nue/tokenizer/BPETokenizer.hx::rankOf`\n- `nue/nue/tokenizer/BPETokenizer.hx::specialId`\n- `nue/nue/tokenizer/Vocab.hx::lookup`\n\nThe downside is the double-hash; the upside is it sidesteps the\ncompiler bug entirely.\n\n\nPick one of:\n\n1. **MIR-lower `StringMap.get` to an `exists` gate + boxed result.**\n   Emit roughly `exists(k) ? haxe_box_int(get_raw(k)) : null`. The\n   `Null\u003cInt\u003e` semantics fall out naturally and existing user code\n   compares against `null` correctly. Requires the runtime to keep\n   the raw `get` callable (rename to `_get_raw`?) and the MIR\n   lowering to insert the conditional wrap.\n\n2. **Replace the extern with a Haxe wrapper.** Make `StringMap.hx`\n   own a Haxe-level `get(key)` that calls `_get_raw` + `exists` and\n   returns `Null\u003cT\u003e` explicitly. Cleaner from the user's POV; but\n   `StringMap` is currently fully `extern class`, so making part of\n   it Haxe-level needs a small refactor.\n\n3. **Return a sentinel for missing.** Doesn't work for arbitrary T —\n   any sentinel could be a valid stored value.\n\n(1) is the cleanest. (2) is the most surgical. Both will need\nmatching changes for IntMap / ObjectMap / EnumValueMap so the fix\nisn't piecemeal.\n\n\nWhen you see `var x = m.get(key); if (x == null) ...` in Haxe code\nrunning on Rayzor today, **don't trust it.** Use `if (m.exists(key))\n... else ...` instead. Once the proper fix above lands, the natural\nHaxe idiom will work and the workarounds in nue can be removed.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_stringmap_null_get` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}